## Final Judgement
### Step 1: Summary of Key Insights
The official overall All India Topper (AIR 1) for the NEET 2020 examination was Soyeb Aftab, who scored a perfect 720/720 and did not fail. However, prominent news outlets reported on a major blunder where another candidate, Mridul Rawat, was initially issued a scorecard showing he had failed, but after a recheck, he was declared the All India Topper in the Scheduled Tribe (ST) category. In contrast, the National Testing Agency (NTA) released official statements strongly denying these reports, calling the claims of grading errors and the news about the ST topper "fake" and "fabricated," and maintaining that the declared results were correct and unchanged. 

### Step 2: Lacking Information
To conclusively resolve the discrepancy, we lack independent, third-party verification of the original physical scorecard issued to Mridul Rawat or an official legal resolution confirming whether the NTA indeed made and corrected a technical error or if the student's claims were indeed fabricated.

### Step 3: Conclusion
Based on the recorded evidence, there is a direct contradiction between the sources. While media reports support the claim by detailing a blunder where the ST category topper was initially declared as failed, the NTA officially refuted these reports as fake and fabricated. Additionally, the actual overall All India Topper (Soyeb Aftab) never failed, meaning the claim's reference to "the all India topper" is at best a misleading reference to a category-specific topper. Because there is substantial supporting and refuting evidence from multiple sources regarding the occurrence of this blunder, the final decision is `conflicting evidence`.

### Verdict: CONFLICTING

### Justification
The official overall All India Topper (AIR 1) for the NEET 2020 examination was Soyeb Aftab, who scored a perfect 720/720 and did not fail, as documented by [Careers360](https://medicine.careers360.com/articles/neet-2021-toppers). However, media outlets like [Livemint](https://www.livemint.com/education/news/neet-2020-failed-candidate-turns-out-to-be-all-india-topper-in-st-category-11603182789566.html) and [Oneindia](https://www.oneindia.com/india/neet-2020-when-a-topper-was-declared-failed-3165796.html) reported on a major blunder where another candidate, Mridul Rawat, was initially issued a scorecard showing he had failed, but after a recheck, was declared the All India Topper in the Scheduled Tribe (ST) category. Conversely, the National Testing Agency (NTA) released official statements reported by [NDTV](https://www.ndtv.com/education/claims-of-errors-in-neet-2020-results-fake-fabricated-nta-2313136) and [Livemint](https://www.livemint.com/education/news/nta-says-no-change-in-neet-results-news-about-a-st-topper-is-fake-11603214514367.html) strongly denying these reports, calling the claims of grading errors "fake" and "fabricated" and maintaining that the declared results were correct and unchanged. Due to these directly contradicting accounts from prominent media reports and the official agency, the final verdict is conflicting evidence.